= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose a common origin at vertex $C$, set $\mathbf a=\overrightarrow{CB}$ and $\mathbf b=\overrightarrow{CA}$, and orient the remaining edge as $\mathbf c=\overrightarrow{AB}$. Then
$$
\boxed{\mathbf c=\mathbf a-\mathbf b.}
$$
This convention makes the interior angle between $\mathbf a$ and $\mathbf b$ equal to $C$, and their lengths equal to the opposite side lengths $a$ and $b$.

\Image[paper-2-triangle-vectors.png]
{title=Consistently oriented triangle edge vectors for the sine and cosine rules}
{description=An original illustrative triangle with two vectors from vertex $C$ and their difference from $A$ to $B$. Side labels are opposite the corresponding vertices.}

Take the <dot product> of the difference with itself. Since $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=ab\cos C$, the <law of cosines> follows:
$$
\boxed{c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos C.}
$$
The magnitude of the <cross product> of two edge vectors is twice the triangle's <area> $K$. Applying this at each vertex gives $2K=ab\sin C=bc\sin A=ca\sin B$. Divide by the nonzero product $abc$ to obtain the <law of sines>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sin A}{a}=\frac{\sin B}{b}=\frac{\sin C}{c}.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\Omega$ be the interior of the <tetrahedron>, and take any constant <vector field> $\mathbf q$. Its <divergence> is zero, so the <divergence theorem> gives
$$
\mathbf q\cdot\sum_{i=1}^4\mathbf V_i=\sum_{i=1}^4\int_{f_i}\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf n_i\,dS=\int_\Omega\nabla\cdot\mathbf q\,dV=0.
$$
Choosing $\mathbf q$ to be each Cartesian basis vector proves $\sum_i\mathbf V_i=\mathbf0$. This is the general fact that <vector areas of a closed polyhedron sum to zero>, specialized to four faces. The outward orientations are essential: the scalar face areas are all positive and do not sum to zero.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <square wave> alternates between a low interval of length five and a high interval of length five; its <integral average> is three. In the real <Fourier series> convention
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty\left[a_n\cos\frac{n\pi x}{5}+b_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right],
$$
integration over one period gives
$$
a_0=\frac15\int_{-5}^5 f(x)\,dx=6,\qquad a_n=\frac65\int_0^5\cos\frac{n\pi x}{5}\,dx=0,
$$
and
$$
b_n=\frac65\int_0^5\sin\frac{n\pi x}{5}\,dx=\frac6{n\pi}[1-(-1)^n].
$$
Thus only odd <harmonics> occur:
$$
\boxed{f(x)\sim3+\frac{12}{\pi}\sum_{j=0}^\infty\frac{\sin((2j+1)\pi x/5)}{2j+1}.}
$$
The <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> makes the sum equal to the function at its continuity points and to the mean of its one-sided limits at a jump. Consequently **the sum is 3 at each of $x=-5,0,5$**, regardless of any separately assigned values of the original function at those endpoints.

\Image[paper-2-square-wave-fourier.png]
{title=A periodic square wave and its Fourier approximations}
{description=Original plot of the alternating low and high intervals and two partial Fourier sums. The jump values of the Fourier series are their midpoint; finite sums exhibit Gibbs oscillations.}

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
