= Solution
{parent=3}

For <orthogonal matrices> $Q_1,\ldots,Q_m$ of the same size, transpose reverses the order of multiplication. Therefore
$$
(Q_1\cdots Q_m)^T(Q_1\cdots Q_m)=Q_m^T\cdots Q_1^TQ_1\cdots Q_m=I,
$$
by canceling the adjacent pairs $Q_j^TQ_j=I$ successively. Hence every finite product is an <orthogonal matrix>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The given <symmetric matrices> satisfy $E_j^TE_j=E_j^2=I$, so both are <orthogonal matrices>. Put $J=E_1E_2$, $c=\cos(\theta/2)$ and $s=\sin(\theta/2)$. Anticommutation gives
$$
J^T=E_2E_1=-J,\qquad J^2=E_1E_2E_1E_2=-I.
$$
Thus $A=cI+sJ$ and $A^T=cI-sJ$, giving $AA^T=(c^2+s^2)I=I$. These are <orthogonal rotations generated by anticommuting involutions>. Also $A-A^T=2sJ$. Since $J^2=-I$ makes $J$ invertible, **$A$ is symmetric exactly when $\sin(\theta/2)=0$**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use $JE_1=-E_2$, $E_1J=E_2$ and $JE_1J=E_1$ to expand the <matrix product>:
$$
AE_1A^T=c^2E_1+cs(JE_1-E_1J)-s^2JE_1J=(c^2-s^2)E_1-2csE_2.
$$
The double-angle identities therefore give
$$
\boxed{AE_1A^T=\cos\theta\,E_1-\sin\theta\,E_2.}
$$
Similarly $JE_2=E_1$, $E_2J=-E_1$ and $JE_2J=E_2$, so
$$
\boxed{AE_2A^T=\sin\theta\,E_1+\cos\theta\,E_2.}
$$
This <conjugation> rotates the two-dimensional span of the two matrices through the double angle described by <orthogonal rotations generated by anticommuting involutions>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write the general real <symmetric matrix> as $E_2=\begin{pmatrix}r&t\\t&u\end{pmatrix}$. With the specified $E_1$, anticommutation reads
$$
E_1E_2+E_2E_1=\begin{pmatrix}2t&r+u\\r+u&2t\end{pmatrix}=0.
$$
Hence $t=0$ and $u=-r$. The remaining condition $E_2^2=I$ gives $r^2=1$, leaving exactly
$$
\boxed{E_2=\pm\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both matrices directly satisfy all the conditions.

