= Solution
{parent=a}

The double-angle identity eliminates the parameter: $y=1+\cos2\theta=2\cos^2\theta=2x^2$. As $\theta$ increases from zero to $\pi$, $x$ decreases from one to minus one. The positive <area> below this <parabola> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathcal A=\int_{-1}^1 2x^2\,dx=\frac43.}
$$
Equivalently, the parameter integral is $-\int_0^\pi(1+\cos2\theta)(-\sin\theta)d\theta$. The leading minus sign corrects for traversal from right to left; the signed integral $\int y\,dx$ along that traversal would be negative.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $0<\theta<\pi$, the <chain rule> for a <parametric curve> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{-4\sin4\theta}{-\sin\theta}=16\cos\theta\cos2\theta.}
$$
At $\theta=0$ the quotient of parameter derivatives is formally $0/0$, but the simplified expression has limit $16$. Thus **the limiting tangent at $(x,y)=(1,1)$ has gradient 16**. This is an endpoint limit of the given open parameter interval, or the ordinary tangent of the smooth polynomial extension $y=8x^4-8x^2+1$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The fourth <Chebyshev polynomial> eliminates the parameter:
$$
y=\cos4\theta=8x^4-8x^2+1,\qquad -1<x<1.
$$
Its derivatives are $y'=16x(2x^2-1)$ and $y''=16(6x^2-1)$. The interior <stationary points> are a local maximum $(0,1)$ at $\theta=\pi/2$, and two minima $(\pm1/\sqrt2,-1)$ at $\theta=\pi/4,3\pi/4$. At the maximum $y''=-16<0$, while at either minimum $y''=32>0$.

The two <inflection points> are
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(\pm\frac1{\sqrt6},-\frac19\right),\qquad \theta=\arccos\frac1{\sqrt6},\ \pi-\arccos\frac1{\sqrt6}.}
$$
The curvature changes sign there. The endpoint limits are $(\pm1,1)$, at $\theta=0,\pi$; they are excluded from the open curve. The central maximum and the two minima are also the attained global extremal values on that open curve.

\Image[paper-2-parametric-curves.png]
{title=Parametric curves after eliminating the cosine parameter}
{description=Original plots of the parabola and fourth Chebyshev polynomial on their open parameter domains. Arrows show traversal from right to left; the quartic's extrema and inflection points are marked.}

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the intended model of independent <Bernoulli trials>, with the same scoring probability $p$ for every shot. Equal marginal scoring probabilities alone would not establish the stated formula without independence. Let $N_1,N_2$ count misses in the two groups of five. They are <independent random variables> with <binomial distribution> $\operatorname{Bin}(5,1-p)$. A tie occurs precisely when $N_1=N_2=n$ for one of the mutually exclusive possibilities $n=0,\ldots,5$. Therefore the <tie probability for independent binomial counts> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\sum_{n=0}^5\binom5n^2(1-p)^{2n}p^{10-2n}.}
$$
At $p=1/2$, <Vandermonde's identity> gives $\sum_n\binom5n^2=\binom{10}5=252$, so
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{252}{1024}=\frac{63}{256}.}
$$
Exchanging the two teams preserves the distribution and interchanges the two winning events. Their total probability is $1-\alpha$, so **the first team wins at the end of the initial period with probability $193/512$**. In this model the shooting order gives no advantage.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Each additional pair of independent shots resolves the tie with probability $r=2p(1-p)$, since exactly one team must score. It preserves the tie with probability $q=p^2+(1-p)^2=1-r$. Finishing after $10+2k+2$ shots means an initial tie, then $k$ tied pairs, then a decisive pair. Independence and <geometric continuation after an initial tie> give
$$
\boxed{\Pr(N=12+2k)=\alpha\,[p^2+(1-p)^2]^k\,2p(1-p),\qquad k=0,1,\ldots.}
$$
Because $0<p<1$, $r>0$ and $q<1$. The total eventual winning probability is the sum of winning in the first ten shots and winning later:
$$
(1-\alpha)+\sum_{k=0}^\infty\alpha q^kr=1-\alpha+\frac{\alpha r}{1-q}=1.
$$
Equivalently, conditional on the initial tie, the probability of still being tied after $m$ further pairs is $q^m\to0$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

For the negative-sign equation, take a nonzero separated solution $\phi=X(x)Y(y)$. <Separation of variables> gives $X''=-\mu X$ and $Y''=-(25-\mu)Y$. The zero <Dirichlet boundary conditions> on $X$ force $\mu=m^2$ with a positive integer $m$ and $X=\sin mx$, up to a constant. Zero boundary values on $Y$ similarly require $25-m^2=n^2$ with a positive integer $n$. A zero or negative separation eigenvalue would give linear or hyperbolic factors, whose two zero endpoint values force the trivial solution.

The only positive integer pairs with $m^2+n^2=25$ are $(3,4)$ and $(4,3)$. By the complete <Dirichlet eigensystem of a rectangle>, these span the whole eigenspace, so
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y)=A\sin3x\sin4y+B\sin4x\sin3y,\qquad A,B\in\mathbb R.}
$$
For the positive-sign equation there is **only the zero solution**. One can use the same strictly positive eigenvalues of $-\Delta$, or directly apply the <zero Dirichlet solution of the modified Helmholtz equation>. Multiplying by $\phi$ and using <Green's first identity> gives
$$
25\int_\Omega\phi^2\,dx\,dy=\int_\Omega\phi\Delta\phi\,dx\,dy=-\int_\Omega|\nabla\phi|^2\,dx\,dy,
$$
where $\Omega=(0,\pi)^2$. Both nonnegative integrals must vanish, proving $\boxed{\phi=0}$.

