= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $H(x,y,z)=x^3+xy+yz-1$. The <gradient> $\nabla H=(3x^2+y,x+z,y)$ is a <normal vector> to its <level set>. Since $y\geq1/2$ on the given patch, division by its last component is valid and gives the unique normalized field
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u=\frac{\nabla H}{y}=\left(\frac{3x^2+y}{y},\frac{x+z}{y},1\right).}
$$
Its dot product with $\mathbf k$ is one, as required; it is not generally a unit vector.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Orthogonal projection of a small surface patch onto the $xy$ plane multiplies its scalar area by $\cos\alpha=\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf k>0$. Hence $dx\,dy=\cos\alpha\,dS$ and $d\mathbf S=\mathbf n\,dx\,dy/\cos\alpha$. Since $\mathbf n=\mathbf u/|\mathbf u|$ and $\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf k=1$, one has $\cos\alpha=1/|\mathbf u|$. The <vector area element of an implicit surface> is thus
$$
\boxed{d\mathbf S=\mathbf u\,dx\,dy=\left(\frac{3x^2+y}{y},\frac{x+z}{y},1\right)dx\,dy.}
$$
The positive final component implements the specified upward orientation.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take the <dot product> of the given field with the upward <vector area element>. The surface relation simplifies its integrand:
$$
\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=[y(x+z)+x]dx\,dy=(1-x^3+x)dx\,dy.
$$
The projected domain is exactly the rectangle $1/2\leq x,y\leq1$, since $y>0$ determines a unique $z=(1-x^3)/y-x$. Therefore the <surface integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_{S_1}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\frac12\int_{1/2}^1(1-x^3+x)dx=\frac12\left[x-\frac{x^4}{4}+\frac{x^2}{2}\right]_{1/2}^1=\frac{41}{128}.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The defining function $x^3+xy-1$ is independent of $z$, so a <normal vector> is $(3x^2+y,x,0)$. It never vanishes here because $x>0$. Every normal is horizontal, whereas $\mathbf G=(0,0,x)$ is vertical and tangent to the surface. Their <dot product> is zero at every point, giving
$$
\boxed{\int_{S_2}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf S=0.}
$$
This is independent of orientation. Although the surface extends without a bound on $x$, the integrand vanishes identically, so no convergence problem arises for this flux.

