= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the <Taylor expansions> $\ln(1+x)=x-x^2/2+x^3/3+O(x^4)$ and $(1-x)^{-1}=1+x+x^2+O(x^3)$. Collecting the first three nonzero coefficients gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\ln(1+x)}{1-x}=x+\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{5x^3}{6}+O(x^4).}
$$
The cubic coefficient is $1-1/2+1/3=5/6$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <geometric series> $(1+u)^{-1}=1-u+u^2+O(u^3)$ with $u=\sin x=x+O(x^3)$. The constant term counts as the first nonzero term, so the requested expansion is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{1+\sin x}=1-x+x^2+O(x^3).}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $\cos x=1+u$ with $u=-x^2/2+x^4/24-x^6/720+O(x^8)$. Substitution into $\ln(1+u)=u-u^2/2+u^3/3+O(u^4)$ gives
$$
\ln\cos x=-\frac{x^2}{2}+\left(\frac1{24}-\frac18\right)x^4+\left(-\frac1{720}+\frac1{48}-\frac1{24}\right)x^6+O(x^8).
$$
Thus the first three nonzero terms of this even <Taylor series> are
$$
\boxed{\ln\cos x=-\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{x^4}{12}-\frac{x^6}{45}+O(x^8).}
$$
The real logarithm is well defined near zero because $\cos x>0$ there.

