= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the fixed exponents $p,q$ in the original PDF. Assume positive $a,b$ so arbitrary real powers and a relative change are defined. Set $h=k/100$. With $b$ fixed, the exact new-to-old ratio is $(1+h)^p$. The <binomial series>, or the one-variable case of the <second-order relative change of a monomial>, gives
$$
100\frac{\Delta f}{f}=100\left[ph+\frac{p(p-1)}2h^2+O(h^3)\right].
$$
Therefore the approximate percentage change through second order is
$$
\boxed{pk+\frac{p(p-1)}{200}k^2.}
$$
For negative $k$ this same signed expression describes a decrease.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $h=k/100$ and $j=m/100$. The exact ratio is $(1+h)^p(1+j)^q$. Multiplying the two <binomial series> and retaining total degree at most two gives the <second-order relative change of a monomial>:
$$
\frac{\Delta f}{f}=ph+qj+\frac{p(p-1)}2h^2+pqhj+\frac{q(q-1)}2j^2+O((|h|+|j|)^3).
$$
Multiply by one hundred to express the change as a percentage:
$$
\boxed{pk+qm+\frac{p(p-1)k^2+2pqkm+q(q-1)m^2}{200}.}
$$
The cross term accounts for the simultaneous changes in both inputs and cannot be recovered by simply adding their separate second-order percentage changes.

= 10
