= Solution
{parent=i}

The <shifted integral representation of a p-series tail> evaluates the defining integral as
$$
I_p(k)=\frac1{(p-1)(5-k)^{p-1}},\qquad k<5.
$$
It is strictly increasing in $k$, since $I_p'(k)=(5-k)^{-p}>0$. From the bounds just proved with $a=5$, $I_3(0)<\sum_{n=5}^\infty n^{-3}<I_3(1)$. Since the middle expression equals $I_3(k_3)$, monotonicity proves
$$
\boxed{0<k_3<1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract the first four terms from the given full <p-series> sums. Write
$$
S_2=\frac{\pi^2}{6}-\left(1+\frac14+\frac19+\frac1{16}\right)\approx0.2213229557,
$$
and
$$
S_4=\frac{\pi^4}{90}-\left(1+\frac1{16}+\frac1{81}+\frac1{256}\right)\approx0.0035713047.
$$
Solving $I_p(k_p)=S_p$ yields $k_p=5-[(p-1)S_p]^{-1/(p-1)}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{k_2=5-S_2^{-1}\approx0.482,\qquad k_4=5-(3S_4)^{-1/3}\approx0.464.}
$$
The unrounded shifts are approximately $0.4817158633$ and $0.4638861447$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the stipulated interpolation $k_3\approx(k_2+k_4)/2$. The unrounded values give $k_3\approx0.4728010040$, and hence
$$
\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^{-3}\approx1+\frac18+\frac1{27}+\frac1{64}+\frac1{2(5-0.4728010040)^2}\approx1.2020575995.
$$
Thus a sensible reported estimate is
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^{-3}\approx1.20206.}
$$
Using the rounded shifts from part (ii) instead gives $k_3\approx0.473$ and a sum about $1.202059745$, with the same six-significant-figure estimate. The interpolation of shifts is an assumption of this approximation; the <integral test> bounds alone do not prove that many digits of accuracy.

