= Solution
{parent=i}

For $x=e^z>0$, let $Y(z)=y(e^z)$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
y'(x)=\frac{Y'(z)}x,\qquad y''(x)=\frac{Y''(z)-Y'(z)}{x^2}.
$$
Multiplying each original <Cauchy-Euler equation> by $x^2$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\text{(1)}\quad Y''+3Y'+2Y=0,\qquad \alpha=3,\ \beta=2,}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{\text{(2)}\quad Y''+Y=0,\qquad \alpha=0,\ \beta=1.}
$$
The exponential change of variable has made both equations have constant coefficients.

= ii
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For equation (1), the <characteristic equation> is $r^2+3r+2=(r+1)(r+2)=0$, so $y=A/x+B/x^2$. The initial conditions give $A+B=0$ and $-A-2B=1$, whence $A=1$, $B=-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y_1(x)=\frac1x-\frac1{x^2},\qquad x>0.}
$$
For equation (2), the transformed solution is $Y=C\cos z+D\sin z$. At $x=1$ one has $z=0$ and $y'(1)=Y'(0)$. The initial conditions therefore give $C=0$, $D=1$, yielding
$$
\boxed{y_2(x)=\sin(\ln x),\qquad x>0.}
$$
The domain $x>0$ is the regular interval containing the initial point and the one covered by $x=e^z$.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The base costs $ab$, the glass front costs $2ac$, the wooden rear costs $ac$, and the other two walls cost $2bc$. Thus minimize $C=ab+3ac+2bc$ subject to $abc=V>0$. With the <Lagrange multiplier> convention $\nabla C=\lambda\nabla(abc)$, the equations are
$$
b+3c=\lambda bc,\qquad a+2c=\lambda ac,\qquad 3a+2b=\lambda ab.
$$
Multiply these by $a,b,c$, respectively. Comparing their equal right sides gives $ab=3ac=2bc$. Since all dimensions are positive, $a=2c$ and $b=3c$. The volume condition then yields
$$
\boxed{(a,b,c)=(2,3,1)\left(\frac V6\right)^{1/3}.}
$$
This stationary shape is the unique <global minimum>, not merely a candidate: the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> applied to the three cost terms gives
$$
C\geq3[(ab)(3ac)(2bc)]^{1/3}=3(6V^2)^{1/3},
$$
with equality exactly at those ratios. Therefore the least cost, in pounds in the specified units, is
$$
\boxed{C_{\min}=3\,6^{1/3}V^{2/3}=18\left(\frac V6\right)^{2/3}.}
$$
This is a <weighted open-box minimization>, with each pair of opposite walls combined into its appropriate positive face weight.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <stationary point> equations are $x^2+2y-21/4=0$ and $y^2+2x-21/4=0$. Subtracting them factors as $(x-y)(x+y-2)=0$, so all solutions lie either on $x=y$ or on $x+y=2$. The first case gives $x=y=3/2$ or $x=y=-7/2$; the second gives $(x,y)=(-1/2,5/2)$ or $(5/2,-1/2)$. This also verifies the two stated points and exhausts the possible solutions.

The <Hessian matrix> is $H=\begin{pmatrix}2x&2\\2&2y\end{pmatrix}$, with determinant $4(xy-1)$. The <second-derivative test> gives:

* **$(3/2,3/2)$ is a strict local minimum:** $H_{11}=3>0$ and $\det H=5>0$.
* **$(-7/2,-7/2)$ is a strict local maximum:** $H_{11}=-7<0$ and $\det H=45>0$.
* **$(-1/2,5/2)$ and $(5/2,-1/2)$ are saddle points:** $\det H=-9<0$ at both.

These extrema are local. Along $x=y=t$ the cubic term makes the function unbounded above and below, so it has no global maximum or minimum on the whole plane.
